    <!-- 2,000-WORD COMPREHENSIVE TYPING SCIENCE & SEO GUIDE -->
    <article class="prose prose-zinc dark:prose-invert max-w-none border-t border-zinc-200 dark:border-zinc-800 pt-16 space-y-16">
      
      <!-- Section 1: Neurobiology of Touch Typing -->
      <section class="space-y-6">
        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-2 px-3 py-1 rounded-full bg-amber-50 dark:bg-amber-950/40 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-800 text-xs font-mono font-semibold text-amber-700 dark:text-amber-400 uppercase tracking-wider">
          Section 1 · Motor Neuroscience
        </div>
        <h2 class="font-heading font-extrabold text-3xl sm:text-4xl text-zinc-900 dark:text-white tracking-tight">
          The Neurobiology of Touch Typing & Cerebellar Motor Automation
        </h2>
        <p class="text-base sm:text-lg text-zinc-600 dark:text-zinc-300 leading-relaxed">
          Touch typing at high velocities (80 to 140+ WPM) is one of the most sophisticated psychomotor tasks human beings can perform. When novice typists look down at their keyboards, each keystroke requires a conscious, visually guided cognitive feedback loop: visual search, spatial coordinate identification, motor cortex firing, and visual confirmation. This deliberate processing loop caps "hunt-and-peck" typing at roughly 25 to 35 WPM.
        </p>
        <p class="text-base sm:text-lg text-zinc-600 dark:text-zinc-300 leading-relaxed">
          In contrast, proficient touch typists bypass conscious visual control entirely by delegating keystroke sequences to the <strong>cerebellum</strong> and <strong>basal ganglia</strong>—the brain's procedural motor memory hubs. Through hundreds of hours of deliberate practice, letter combinations (n-grams like <em>"the"</em>, <em>"ing"</em>, <em>"tion"</em>, and <em>"ment"</em>) are encoded as unified, ballistically executed motor bursts known as <strong>muscle memory motor chunks</strong>.
        </p>
        
        <div class="p-6 rounded-3xl bg-zinc-50 dark:bg-zinc-900 border border-zinc-200 dark:border-zinc-800 space-y-3">
            <h4 class="font-heading font-bold text-lg text-zinc-900 dark:text-white flex items-center gap-2">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-amber-500 shrink-0" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.75" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M12 5a3 3 0 1 0-5.997.125 4 4 0 0 0-2.526 5.77 4 4 0 0 0 .556 6.588A4 4 0 1 0 12 18Z" /><path d="M12 5a3 3 0 1 1 5.997.125 4 4 0 0 1 2.526 5.77 4 4 0 0 1-.556 6.588A4 4 0 1 1 12 18Z" /><path d="M15 13a4.5 4.5 0 0 1-3-4 4.5 4.5 0 0 1-3 4" /></svg> The Motor Buffer & Lookahead Horizon
            </h4>
          <p class="text-sm text-zinc-600 dark:text-zinc-300 leading-relaxed">
            Eye-tracking neurological studies reveal that elite typists look 2 to 4 words ahead of their active finger movements. The visual cortex buffers upcoming words in working memory while the motor cortex simultaneously executes the physical keystrokes of the current word. This pipeline architecture eliminates pause latency between words, maintaining a continuous, fluid rhythmic cadence.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- Section 2: Gross WPM vs Net WPM -->
      <section class="space-y-6">
        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-2 px-3 py-1 rounded-full bg-amber-50 dark:bg-amber-950/40 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-800 text-xs font-mono font-semibold text-amber-700 dark:text-amber-400 uppercase tracking-wider">
          Section 2 · Psychometric Measurement
        </div>
        <h2 class="font-heading font-extrabold text-3xl sm:text-4xl text-zinc-900 dark:text-white tracking-tight">
          Gross WPM vs. Net WPM: The Scientific Accuracy Formulas
        </h2>
        <p class="text-base sm:text-lg text-zinc-600 dark:text-zinc-300 leading-relaxed">
          Measuring typing speed requires precise mathematical standardization. Because natural language words vary in length from single-letter pronouns ("I", "a") to multi-syllabic terms ("psychometrics"), international typing benchmarks universally standardize <strong>1 word = 5 keystrokes</strong> (including letters, numbers, spaces, and punctuation).
        </p>

        <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-6 pt-2">
          <div class="p-6 rounded-3xl bg-zinc-50 dark:bg-zinc-900 border border-zinc-200 dark:border-zinc-800 space-y-3">
            <h3 class="font-heading font-bold text-xl text-amber-600 dark:text-amber-400">Gross WPM (Raw Velocity)</h3>
            <p class="text-sm font-mono text-zinc-700 dark:text-zinc-300 bg-white dark:bg-zinc-800/80 p-3 rounded-xl border border-zinc-200 dark:border-zinc-700">
              Gross WPM = (Total Keystrokes / 5) / (Time in Minutes)
            </p>
            <p class="text-sm text-zinc-600 dark:text-zinc-400 leading-relaxed">
              Measures pure motor speed irrespective of typos. Useful for diagnosing raw finger twitch capability and mechanical key actuation limits.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="p-6 rounded-3xl bg-zinc-50 dark:bg-zinc-900 border border-zinc-200 dark:border-zinc-800 space-y-3">
            <h3 class="font-heading font-bold text-xl text-amber-600 dark:text-amber-400">Net WPM (True Usable Speed)</h3>
            <p class="text-sm font-mono text-zinc-700 dark:text-zinc-300 bg-white dark:bg-zinc-800/80 p-3 rounded-xl border border-zinc-200 dark:border-zinc-700">
              Net WPM = Gross WPM - (Uncorrected Errors / Time in Minutes)
            </p>
            <p class="text-sm text-zinc-600 dark:text-zinc-400 leading-relaxed">
              Applies a strict error deduction penalty. Net WPM is the clinical standard because uncorrected errors require post-hoc editing, destroying workplace throughput.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <p class="text-base sm:text-lg text-zinc-600 dark:text-zinc-300 leading-relaxed pt-2">
          Furthermore, <strong>Characters Per Minute (CPM)</strong> provides an absolute metric of raw character velocity (`CPM = (Correct Keystrokes / Seconds) * 60`), which is widely utilized across European, Russian, and East Asian typing certification standards.
        </p>
      </section>

      <!-- Section 3: Global Typing Speed Percentiles -->
      <section class="space-y-6">
        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-2 px-3 py-1 rounded-full bg-amber-50 dark:bg-amber-950/40 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-800 text-xs font-mono font-semibold text-amber-700 dark:text-amber-400 uppercase tracking-wider">
          Section 3 · Demographic Benchmarks
        </div>
        <h2 class="font-heading font-extrabold text-3xl sm:text-4xl text-zinc-900 dark:text-white tracking-tight">
          Global Typing Speed Percentiles & Population Benchmarks
        </h2>
        <p class="text-base sm:text-lg text-zinc-600 dark:text-zinc-300 leading-relaxed">
          Where do your typing skills stand compared to the global population? Across millions of recorded standardized tests, human typing speed follows a Gaussian bell-curve distribution with a median of 41.4 WPM:
        </p>

        <!-- Percentiles Table -->
        <div class="overflow-x-auto rounded-3xl border border-zinc-200 dark:border-zinc-800 bg-zinc-50 dark:bg-zinc-900 p-2">
          <table class="w-full text-left border-collapse text-sm">
            <thead>
              <tr class="border-b border-zinc-200 dark:border-zinc-800 text-zinc-500 dark:text-zinc-400 font-mono text-xs uppercase">
                <th class="p-4">Typing Speed (WPM)</th>
                <th class="p-4">Global Percentile</th>
                <th class="p-4">Typist Classification</th>
                <th class="p-4">Typical Profile</th>
